      "explanation": "Multiply each term of the first factor by both terms of the second factor. The product \\(x^2x^3\\) has five factors of \\(x\\), so it equals \\(x^5\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1+x^2)(1-x^3)\n&=1-x^3+x^2-x^2x^3\\\\\n&=1+x^2-x^3-x^5.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe result is \\(1+x^2-x^3-x^5\\), choice D.",
      "id": "1987_AMC_12_Q01_m1",
      "sourceRef": "amc/1987_AMC_12_Q01#1987_AMC_12_Q01_m1",
      "title": "Distribute the factors"
